CAT = "iespVehicles"; SEG = "VEH"
# Units 9 and 10 of Safe Pass: site safety and construction equipment, site safety and construction
# vehicles. Construction Regulations 2013 (vehicles and plant operated only by a competent person aged
# 18 or over unless under supervised training; Schedule 5 CSCS tasks); General Application Regulations
# 2007 Schedule 1 (hand signals: STOP is the right arm pointing up, palm forward).
Q = [
('According to the HSA, what causes the greatest number of workplace deaths in Ireland?',
 'Accidents involving workplace vehicles', ['Electric shocks from hand tools', 'Burns from welding and hot works', 'Cuts from saws and hand tools'],
 'The HSA notes that accidents involving workplace vehicles cause the greatest number of workplace deaths, which is why traffic management matters on sites.'),
('A 17-year-old labourer is asked to move a site dumper on his own. What does the law say?',
 'Only a competent person aged 18 or over may', ['He may if he holds a car licence', 'He may if the foreman watches', 'He may on Saturdays only'],
 'The Construction Regulations 2013 say vehicles and earthmoving plant must be operated only by a competent person aged 18 or over, unless under supervised training.'),
('Which card shows an operator has completed training for certain plant, such as a tower crane or excavator?',
 'A CSCS card', ['A Safe Pass card', 'A PPS card', 'A first aid card'],
 'Schedule 5 of the Construction Regulations 2013 lists the CSCS tasks, including tower crane, mobile crane, telescopic handler, 180 and 360 degree excavator and dumper operation.'),
('A site plans its traffic routes. Which arrangement protects people on foot best?',
 'Separate walkways with physical barriers', ['Shared routes with a speed sign', 'Workers wearing headphones', 'Vehicles using their horns often'],
 'Separating people from vehicles with barriers and marked walkways is the most effective traffic control. Signs and horns only warn.'),
('Why is reversing a large vehicle on site so dangerous?',
 'The driver has large blind spots', ['It uses much more diesel', 'It wears out the tyres faster', 'It confuses the satellite navigation'],
 'Drivers cannot see directly behind or close beside many site vehicles. A trained signaller, cameras and one-way systems reduce reversing.'),
('What does a signaller (banksman) do?',
 'Guides plant movements safely', ["Collects the site's waste", 'Checks Safe Pass cards at the gate', 'Drives the delivery trucks'],
 'A signaller directs vehicle and crane movements and keeps people clear. They must wear distinctive clothing so the operator can recognise them.'),
('Where should a signaller stand while guiding a reversing truck?',
 "In the driver's view, out of the vehicle's path", ['Directly behind the truck, close in', "Inside the truck's cab, right beside the driver", "Standing on the truck's side step"],
 'The signaller must be visible to the driver at all times and in a safe position. If the driver loses sight of the signaller, the driver must stop.'),
('Which standard hand signal means stop?',
 'Right arm up, palm facing forward', ['Both hands clasped at chest height', 'Left arm pointing out to the side', 'Both arms bent with the palms up'],
 'Under the signals in the General Application Regulations, stop is the right arm pointing upwards with the palm forward. Hands clasped at chest height means end of operation.'),
('A driver loses sight of the signaller while reversing. What must the driver do?',
 'Stop at once', ['Keep going slowly', 'Sound the horn and continue', 'Speed up to finish'],
 'If the operator cannot see the signaller or cannot be sure the move is safe, they must stop and wait for new instructions.'),
('May a worker ride in the bucket of an excavator to reach a high spot?',
 'No, never', ['Yes, if they hold on', 'Yes, for short distances', 'Yes, if the PSCS agrees'],
 'Buckets and forks are not designed to carry people. A fall or sudden movement can kill. Use a mobile elevating work platform instead.'),
('Why must dumper drivers wear a seat belt where one is fitted?',
 'To stay inside the roll-over protection', ['To get through a Garda checkpoint', 'To keep the seat clean and dry', 'To stop the engine from stalling'],
 'Regulation 38 of the General Application Regulations requires roll-over protection on ride-on mobile equipment and a restraining system where the operator could be crushed if it rolls. The protection only works if the belt keeps the driver inside it.'),
('What is a common cause of dumpers overturning?',
 'Driving across or near slopes and edges', ['Driving in a straight line on the level', 'Carrying no load at all', 'Using the handbrake when parked'],
 'Dumpers tip on slopes, at excavation edges and on soft ground. Use stop blocks at tipping points and keep to safe routes.'),
('Before leaving a vehicle on site, what should the driver do?',
 'Lower attachments, brake on, key out', ['Leave the engine running', 'Raise the bucket fully into the air', 'Leave the key in for the next driver'],
 'A parked machine must be safe and secure: attachments on the ground, brake on, engine off and key removed so nobody can start it.'),
('What does a flashing amber beacon on a site machine mean?',
 'The machine is working, keep clear', ['The machine is running out of fuel', 'The driver is away on a break', 'The machine is for hire or for sale'],
 'Beacons warn that plant is moving or working. Stay out of its working area unless the operator knows you are there.'),
("Why must people keep out of an excavator's slewing area?",
 'The rear can swing round and crush them', ['The arm sprays out hydraulic oil', 'The tracks throw up loose stones', 'The cab door can swing outward suddenly on them'],
 'The counterweight swings as the machine slews and can trap a person against another object. Barriers or a signaller keep people out.'),
('What must be checked on lifting equipment such as chains and slings before use?',
 'That it is marked and free from damage', ['Its colour and its length only', 'That it is brand new from the box', 'That it is the lightest one available on site'],
 'The General Application Regulations require lifting accessories to be marked so the characteristics essential for safe use can be identified, and to be thoroughly examined at set intervals. Damaged ones are taken out of use.'),
('A crane is lifting steel beams over the area where a crew wants to keep working. What should happen?',
 'The crew moves clear until the lift is done', ['They keep working with hard hats on', 'They work under it if the load is small', 'They hold the beam by hand to guide it'],
 'A hard hat will not protect against a falling load. Keep clear of suspended loads and use tag lines to steady them from a safe position.'),
('What is the safe working load (SWL) of lifting equipment?',
 'The most it may lift safely', ['The weight of the equipment itself', 'The load it can lift in an hour', 'The lightest load it can handle'],
 'Regulation 48 requires machinery for lifting loads to be clearly marked with its safe working load and not used beyond it except when tested under a competent person.'),
('A guard is missing from a bench saw. What should a worker do?',
 'Not use it and report it', ['Use it with extra care', 'Wear gloves and carry on', 'Ask a colleague to hold the timber'],
 'Machinery guards prevent contact with moving parts. The saw must not be used until the guard is refitted by a competent person.'),
('What should be worn when using an angle grinder?',
 'Eye protection, with the guard fitted', ['Loose gloves and no safety glasses', 'A tie to keep the shirt tidy', 'Shorts and a T-shirt for comfort'],
 'Disc fragments and sparks cause serious eye injuries. Keep the guard on, use the correct disc and wear eye protection; loose clothing can catch.'),
('How often must lifting accessories such as chains, hooks and shackles be thoroughly examined?',
 'At least every 6 months', ['At least every 5 years', 'Only when they are new', 'Only after an accident'],
 'Schedule 1 of the General Application Regulations 2007 sets a thorough examination by a competent person at least every 6 months for lifting accessories such as chains, ropes, hooks and shackles.'),
('A crane must be thoroughly examined by a competent person at least how often?',
 'Every 12 months', ['Every 5 years', 'Every 10 years', 'Only when first bought'],
 'Schedule 1 of the General Application Regulations 2007 sets a thorough examination of a crane at least every 12 months, alongside its regular checks.'),
('A hoist is repaired in a way that affects its safe operation. What must happen before it is used again?',
 'A competent person must examine it', ['A labourer test-lifts a full load', 'The repairer signs the site diary only', 'Nothing, if the hoist runs smoothly'],
 'Regulation 52 of the General Application Regulations requires lifting equipment to be examined by a competent person before returning to service after a repair or alteration relevant to its safe operation.'),
('How should a delivery truck driver be managed on arrival at a site?',
 'Met and directed under the traffic plan', ['Left to find their own way around', 'Told to reverse into the nearest gap', 'Allowed to park on the footpath'],
 'Site traffic management plans set the routes, unloading areas and signaller arrangements for deliveries so drivers and workers are kept safe.'),
]
